here is a compile-time door only and no contract validates a value at run time.

## What this crate is

This crate declares the contracts a numeral satisfies, so that a function generic over a numeral
bounds on a contract rather than on a value type. A consumer that bounds on them depends on this crate
and nothing else: no carrier, no placement, no strategy, no value. `arvo-numeral` implements every
contract for `Num<S, C>`; a crate of a consumer's own implements them for a type of its own on the
same terms.

It depends on `arvo-format` alone, for `Bool` and `Width`, and on `core::cmp::Ordering`. Nothing in
this workspace other than `arvo-numeral` depends on it yet.

## Where it sits

By `arvo-bridge-home-rule` a contract lives in the lowest crate reaching every type in its surface,
and for every contract here that is `arvo-format`. This crate sits directly above `arvo-format`
instead, and that is a deviation from the rule's letter, recorded here with its ground,
`proposal::a_value_contract_is_not_the_format_crates_subject`, at two experts on a provisional
stamp. A contract a value implements is not the subject of the crate that declares the signature the
value satisfies: `arvo-format` holds the representable set, its predicate and the exact operations over
slots, and the types implementing a value contract reach past the format concept. Under
`a-crate-holds-only-its-own-thing` what a crate holds is decided by what it is for, so the contracts
take a crate of their own, and reach then puts that crate as low as it can go, directly above
`arvo-format`. Nothing here names a crate above itself.

## Const traits, and what a consumer gets from them

Every contract with a method is a const trait, and `const_trait_impl` is on unconditionally in this
crate and in `arvo-numeral`. The reason is arvo's own generic code: a generic `const fn` over a
contract exists only under a const trait, and arvo's later families build on such functions. Under a
plain trait none can exist, and under a const trait behind a feature its constness would depend on
whether some crate in the build turned the feature on.

A consumer naming no feature bounds on a contract with a plain bound, `T: Sum`, and calls through it at
run time. It does not evaluate a contract at build time: a call through a contract in a const context
is refused with E0658 in a crate that does not take the gate, whatever the arm (sketch
`mock/research/sketches/202611192130_numeral-contracts-reading/`, probes
`concrete-const-call-no-gate`, `ungated-call-of-gated-generic`, `ungated-runtime-call-control`). A value
at build time comes from the value's own inherent `const fn`s, and a family that needs a build-time
entry point gives it as a concrete function.

## The contracts

Each method is total on the instance's own members under the instance's completion. Each law is the
oracle equality for that operation: the member the method returns is the member the format's own
function gives for the operands, which for `Num` is `arvo_format::add`, `sub`, `neg` and `mul` over
slots. The contracts do not expose a slot or a format, so a generic suite states the law through a
test-side vocabulary each instance supplies, and the law is stated per debug check: a value whose
check aborts a debug build when a result leaves the range does that through a contract as it does
through the inherent method.

| Contract | Surface |
|---|---|
| `Sum` | `sum(self, Self) -> Self`, `fits_sum(self, Self) -> Bool` |
| `Difference` | `difference(self, Self) -> Self`, `fits_difference(self, Self) -> Bool` |
| `Product` | `product(self, Self) -> Self`, `fits_product(self, Self) -> Bool` |
| `Order` | `order(self, Self) -> Ordering`, and provided `lt`, `le`, `gt`, `ge` returning `Bool`, `min`, `max` |
| `Identity<M>` | `const IDENTITY: Self`, with `Additive` and `Multiplicative` the two `M` declared here |
| `Sign` | `is_negative`, `is_zero`, `is_positive`, each `(self) -> Bool` |
| `Magnitude` | `magnitude(self) -> Self` |
| `Constant<const V: i128>` | `const MEMBER: Self` |
| `MonotoneSum` | a marker over `Sum + Order` |
| `MonotoneCompletion` | a marker an overflow policy carries |
| `Reciprocal` | `reciprocal(Self::Nonzero) -> Self`, with `type Nonzero` |
| `SquareRoot` | `square_root(self) -> Self` |

`fits_sum`, `fits_difference` and `fits_product` answer whether the exact result is a member, before
the completion is consulted, so a caller can tell the completed answer from the exact one without a
check of its own.

`Order` is a total order over the members, and it is the order of the members' representatives on the
ambient line, which is the sentence `law::existence_of_a_translation_invariant_total_order` says the
canon owes: under wrap that order exists and is total, and it is not invariant under translation.

`Sign` and `Identity<Additive>` go together: a format without a zero member has no sign, and the
instance refuses at build where it has none. An unsigned instance answers `is_negative` false for every
member.

`Magnitude` gives the absolute value under the instance's completion. Under wrap the least member of a
signed format has no positive counterpart, and its magnitude is itself, which is negative; the law states
that member apart.

`Constant<V>` is the member at slot index `V`. A `V` naming no member is refused at build, as a failed
constant after monomorphisation, so `cargo check` alone does not catch it, and the bound carries no
promise a caller can read earlier than a build. `V` is a slot index rather than a value. The integer at
the const parameter is the ruling's excepted position; a parameter of the stack's own type builds with
no consumer gate (probe `adt-param-consumer`), and it would need `arvo-format`'s `Slot` to be a const
parameter type, which is a change to that crate's design and not this crate's to make.

`MonotoneSum` says that `a <= b` gives `a + c <= b + c` for every `c`. It is selected by the overflow
policy rather than chosen per type: an instance implements it where its policy carries
`MonotoneCompletion`. `Saturate` carries it and `Wrap` does not, since under wrap no translation-invariant
total order exists at any width. An outside policy that saturates carries it from its own crate. A
selection by equality to `Saturate` would refuse such a policy (probe `monotone-outside-policy`), and the
policy-side marker builds and refuses `Wrap` (probes `monotone-policy-marker`,
`monotone-policy-marker-wrap`). No law row yet states the region where monotonicity holds under
saturation; that is ticket `00776298ed63`.

`Reciprocal` takes its operand at a type of the instance's choosing, `Nonzero`, at which zero cannot be
met, as division takes a `Divisor`. A reciprocal over every member would meet zero, and meeting it
would take a check at run time or a completion nobody has declared. `Reciprocal` and `SquareRoot` are
named here with no instance, and no law until the families that implement them.

Every associated item is typed in `Self`, `Bool`, `Ordering` or `Width`, and a bare integer stands only
at a const generic parameter.

## What is proved, and how

The law suite and the consumer crate live with the instance, in `arvo-numeral`, since a contract
without an instance has nothing to test. This crate's own tests are the compile-fail tests of its
surface.

## What is not here

The bit tier, width, access, logic and sequence over `Bits<N>`, is the `arvo-bits` round's, with its
impls in `arvo-bits`. Division and conversion as contracts, the warm tier's overflow and rounding, and
capacity are later steps. No contract validates a value at run time.
